Below is a practical, step‑by‑step guide you can copy‑paste into your project to give any form field a “live‑typing” animation when the user focuses it (or when you want to pre‑populate a field with a typing effect).  
The solution uses the **SVG‑based typing animation** that the `functions` API provides, so you get a smooth, resolution‑independent effect without any heavy JavaScript libraries.

---

## 1. Overview of the workflow

| Step | What happens | API call |
|------|--------------|----------|
| **A** | Create an SVG that contains the text you want to animate. | `createTypingSVG` |
| **B** | (Optional) Fine‑tune the speed and the initial delay. | `setTypingAnimation` |
| **C** | Insert the generated SVG into the DOM next to the `<input>`/`<textarea>`. | plain DOM manipulation |
| **D** | When the user focuses the field, hide the SVG and show the real input (or vice‑versa). | plain JS event listeners |
| **E** | (Optional) Export the SVG as PNG/JPG for fallback or caching. | `exportTypingSVG` |

---

## 2. Create the typing SVG

```js
// 1️⃣  Define the text you want to appear as if it is being typed.
const placeholderText = "Enter your email address…";

// 2️⃣  Call the SVG‑creation function.
//    You can adjust font, colour, size and typing speed here.
const svgResponse = await functions.createTypingSVG({
  text: placeholderText,
  fontFamily: "Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif",
  fontSize: 16,
  fontColor: "#555555",
  typingSpeed: 80   // words‑per‑minute (default 60)
});

// The API returns an object that contains the SVG markup and a generated ID.
const { svgMarkup, svgId } = svgResponse;
```

> **Tip:** If you want a slower “human‑like” feel, drop the `typingSpeed` to 40‑50 wpm. Faster UI feedback can be achieved with 100‑120 wpm.

---

## 3. (Optional) Adjust delay or speed after creation

If you need a pause before the animation starts—e.g., you only want the typing to begin after the user clicks the field—use `setTypingAnimation`:

```js
await functions.setTypingAnimation({
  delayBeforeStart: 500,   // half‑second pause
  typingSpeed: 70          // override the speed if you like
});
```

> **Note:** The `setTypingAnimation` call affects the *next* SVG you create, so call it **before** `createTypingSVG` if you want the settings to apply to that SVG.

---

## 4. Insert the SVG into the page

```html
<!-- Your form field -->
<div class="form‑field‑wrapper" style="position:relative;">
  <input id="emailInput" type="email" placeholder="" style="opacity:0; position:relative; z-index:2;">
  <!-- The SVG will be placed here -->
  <div id="emailPlaceholder" style="position:absolute; top:0; left:0; z-index:1;"></div>
</div>
```

```js
// 4️⃣  Put the SVG markup into the placeholder container.
document.getElementById('emailPlaceholder').innerHTML = svgMarkup;

// 5️⃣  When the user focuses the input, hide the SVG and show the real field.
const input = document.getElementById('emailInput');
const placeholderDiv = document.getElementById('emailPlaceholder');

input.addEventListener('focus', () => {
  // Fade out the SVG (you can also just remove it)
  placeholderDiv.style.transition = 'opacity 0.2s';
  placeholderDiv.style.opacity = '0';

  // Reveal the real input
  input.style.opacity = '1';
  input.focus();
});
```

**Result:**  
When the page loads, the user sees the placeholder text being typed out. As soon as they click the field, the animation fades away and the real `<input>` becomes active.

---

## 5. Re‑use the same SVG for multiple fields

If you have several fields that share the same placeholder style, you can generate **one** SVG and clone it:

```js
function attachTypingPlaceholder(inputId, text) {
  // 1️⃣ Create (or reuse) the SVG
  const { svgMarkup, svgId } = await functions.createTypingSVG({
    text,
    fontFamily: "sans-serif",
    fontSize: 14,
    fontColor: "#777",
    typingSpeed: 70
  });

  // 2️⃣ Insert it next to the input
  const wrapper = document.createElement('div');
  wrapper.style.position = 'relative';
  const input = document.getElementById(inputId);
  input.parentNode.insertBefore(wrapper, input);
  wrapper.appendChild(input);               // keep the input inside the wrapper
  const placeholder = document.createElement('div');
  placeholder.innerHTML = svgMarkup;
  placeholder.style.position = 'absolute';
  placeholder.style.top = '0';
  placeholder.style.left = '0';
  placeholder.style.zIndex = '1';
  wrapper.appendChild(placeholder);

  // 3️⃣ Hide/show logic
  input.style.opacity = '0';
  input.addEventListener('focus', () => {
    placeholder.style.opacity = '0';
    input.style.opacity = '1';
  });
}
```

Call it for each field:

```js
attachTypingPlaceholder('emailInput',  "Enter your email address…");
attachTypingPlaceholder('nameInput',   "Your full name");
attachTypingPlaceholder('messageInput',"Write your message here…");
```

---

## 6. Exporting the SVG (optional fallback)

If you need a static image for browsers that don’t support inline SVG (very rare nowadays) you can export it:

```js
const exportResult = await functions.exportTypingSVG({
  format: "png",
  svgId   // the ID you received from createTypingSVG
});
// exportResult contains a data‑URL you can use as a fallback <img src="…">
```

---

## 7. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Form with typing placeholder</title>
  <style>
    .field-wrapper { position:relative; margin:1rem 0; }
    .field-wrapper input,
    .field-wrapper textarea { width:100%; padding:0.5rem; font-size:1rem; }
    .placeholder-svg { position:absolute; top:0; left:0; pointer-events:none; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="field-wrapper">
  <input id="emailInput" type="email" placeholder="">
  <div id="emailPlaceholder" class="placeholder-svg"></div>
</div>

<script type="module">
  // ---- 1️⃣  Create the SVG ----
  const { svgMarkup, svgId } = await functions.createTypingSVG({
    text: "Enter your email address…",
    fontFamily: "Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif",
    fontSize: 16,
    fontColor: "#555",
    typingSpeed: 80
  });

  // ---- 2️⃣  Insert it ----
  document.getElementById('emailPlaceholder').innerHTML = svgMarkup;

  // ---- 3️⃣  Hide/show on focus ----
  const input = document.getElementById('emailInput');
  const placeholder = document.getElementById('emailPlaceholder');

  input.style.opacity = '0';               // start hidden
  input.addEventListener('focus', () => {
    placeholder.style.opacity = '0';
    input.style.opacity = '1';
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

---

## 8. Things to keep in mind

| Consideration | Why it matters | Quick fix |
|---------------|----------------|-----------|
| **Accessibility** | Screen‑readers ignore SVG text by default. Keep a proper `<label>` and a real `placeholder` attribute (or `aria-label`) on the `<input>` so assistive tech still knows the purpose. | Add `aria-label="Email address"` to the input. |
| **Responsive fonts** | The SVG uses a fixed `fontSize`. If you have a responsive design, recalculate the size on `window.resize`. | Call `createTypingSVG` again with a new `fontSize` when the breakpoint changes. |
| **Multiple animations** | Each SVG runs its own timer. Too many simultaneous animations can cause a tiny CPU bump. | Limit the number of visible placeholders (e.g., only animate the first field the user scrolls to). |
| **Form reset** | When the form is cleared, you may want the placeholder to re‑type. | Call `setTypingAnimation` + `createTypingSVG` again on the reset event. |

---

## 9. TL;DR – One‑liner for a single field

```js
const {svgMarkup}=await functions.createTypingSVG({text:"Your name",typingSpeed:70});
document.getElementById('namePlaceholder').innerHTML=svgMarkup;
document.getElementById('nameInput').addEventListener('focus',e=>{
  e.target.style.opacity='1';
  document.getElementById('namePlaceholder').style.opacity='0';
});
```

That’s all you need to give users a polished, “typing‑as‑you‑fill” experience on any form element. Feel free to tweak the colors, fonts, and speeds to match your brand’s visual language. Happy coding! 🚀

